<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mahmud Shah Bahmani</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 1482–1518</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Shihab-ud-Din Mahmud</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mahmud Shah was sultan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate</a> from 1482 to 1518, coming to the throne as a boy after the death of Muhammad Shah III. Real power passed to the nobles, above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qasim-barid">Qasim Barid</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bidar">Bidar</a>, while the provincial governors of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmadnagar">Ahmadnagar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berar">Berar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golconda-fort">Golconda</a> made themselves independent. By his death the sultanate had broken into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan-sultanates">Deccan sultanates</a>.</p></div>
